-- Conformance: == / Eq on a non-Eq-derivable type is a checker error (Chapter 9, Section 9.8.1)
-- Tests: E243 — the Eq ability's `==` (and `eq`) operation is rejected on a
-- non-Eq-derivable operand (#928, the equality sibling of #921).  Eq derives
-- STRUCTURALLY (§9.8.2): the Eq primitives, simple enums, and ADTs whose fields
-- are (recursively) all Eq — but a field with no Eq semantics (Array / Map /
-- Set / a function / a Tuple) makes the whole type non-derivable.  `Rec` here
-- has a `Map` field, so it is not Eq-derivable.  This is the exact silent
-- wrong result #928 closes: without this gate a non-Eq `==` type-checked, then
-- codegen fell to a raw i32/pointer comparison that never reached the
-- structural-Eq derivability dispatch (identity, not value equality).  The
-- checker now rejects it, keeping check, codegen, and verify in agreement.
private data Rec {
  MkRec(Map<String, Int>)
}

public fn main(@Unit -> @Bool)
  requires(true)
  ensures(true)
  effects(pure)
{
  let @Rec = MkRec(map_new());
  let @Rec = MkRec(map_new());
  @Rec.0 == @Rec.1
}
